        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>Understanding how devices negotiate physical link properties is critical for resolving low-level <a href="packet-loss-troubleshooting.html">packet loss</a> and performance issues.</p>
            
            <h3>Duplex Modes</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Half Duplex:</strong> A device can send OR receive data at any given time, but not both simultaneously. Often associated with legacy hubs or highly congested Wi-Fi.</li>
                <li><strong>Full Duplex:</strong> A device can send AND receive data simultaneously. This is the standard for modern switched Ethernet.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Auto-Negotiation</h3>
            <p>Ethernet auto-negotiation allows two connected devices to agree on the best possible speed and duplex mode. In modern switched Ethernet networks, full-duplex with auto-negotiation enabled is typically preferred and expected.</p>
            
            <h3>The Duplex Mismatch</h3>
            <p>A classic duplex mismatch occurs when one side of a link is manually hardcoded (e.g., to 100/Full) while the other side is left to auto-negotiate. The auto-negotiating side can usually detect the speed, but cannot detect the duplex, falling back to a safe default of Half-Duplex.</p>
            
            <p><strong>Symptoms of a mismatch include:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Extremely poor throughput (while basic pings might work fine).</li>
                <li>Collisions or late-collision counters incrementing on legacy or shared Ethernet interfaces.</li>
                <li>Frame errors and heavy TCP retransmissions.</li>
                <li>General performance instability.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Modern Accuracy</h3>
            <p>Duplex mismatches are significantly less common on modern infrastructure. Modern 1 Gbps and higher Ethernet standards mandate auto-negotiation to establish master/slave clocking correctly. While you can sometimes force speed/duplex on 1G interfaces depending on the vendor, disabling auto-negotiation on modern multi-gigabit links is generally discouraged and often unsupported.</p>
        
        </div>
        
